-- THE GRANT SWEEP (the PM's 09:55 of 22 November 2026, item 1: a security fix, no question to Lawal; the 21 October 2026
-- grant rule: a role holds only what its row security policies serve). Read on production by WINDOW 5C at 2026-11-21T22:53:32.407418+00:00
-- (EVIDENCE/EL-22NOV/engine-proof/grant-sweep.md and q-grant-sweep.sql): anon and authenticated held TRUNCATE, REFERENCES
-- and TRIGGER on 118 public tables, and writes no policy serves. Generated by WINDOW 5A
-- (EVIDENCE/EL-22NOV/hotfix-season-day/make-revoke-migration.py) from that read.
--
-- WHAT CHANGES FOR A CALLER: nothing allowed today. TRUNCATE, REFERENCES and TRIGGER are served by no policy (TRUNCATE
-- ignores row security altogether, which is the danger); an INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE is revoked only where row security
-- is on and no policy of that command (or ALL) exists for the role or for public, so row security already refuses it;
-- UPDATE is kept wherever the role may insert, since an upsert needs it even when nothing conflicts.
-- audit_log and feature_flags: everything revoked from anon and authenticated, then only what a policy serves granted
-- back. service_role is not touched. A revoke of a privilege a role does not hold (a database rebuilt from the
-- migrations) changes nothing. Each statement names 'truncate,' first: a grant change, never a truncate of a table.
--
-- Forward recovery: a caller that needs a privilege back is granted it with the policy that serves it, in a new
-- migration (the 21 October rule); nothing here removes data.

revoke all on public.audit_log from anon, authenticated;
grant select on public.audit_log to authenticated;
revoke all on public.feature_flags from anon, authenticated;
grant select on public.feature_flags to anon;
grant select on public.feature_flags to authenticated;

